Resolution #2 Title & Author “Creating the DSA National Growth & Development Committee” by Benjamin D., Natarajan S., & Jacquelyn S. of Metro DC DSA & Jordan F. of New Orleans DSA Budget Analysis Staff Costs $37,305 Some of this work is already planned with the hire of our new Tech and Data Manager Rebecca, Membership Services Associate Elizabeth, and Organizing Director Glenn. But significant staff time would be required to coordinate the whole project - estimated 1/2 time staffer more. 12% Overhead $4,477 Direct Costs $0 Printing is in the existing budget. Total Cost $41,781 Resolve Therefore be it resolved, that the Convention directs the National Political Committee (NPC) to establish the Growth & Development Committee with the explicit purpose of developing, and facilitating the execution of a plan to grow DSA to 100,000 members in good standing by January 1st, 2021; and be it Resolved, that this plan shall include: 1) a national roadmap for growth consisting of specific targets and milestones broken down by region, and 2) a comprehensive recruitment, retention, and leadership development strategy prioritizing growth among black and brown workers, queer and women workers, and working class populations in general; and be it Resolved, that this plan shall furthermore include the development of standard materials and trainings for recruitment, member engagement, leadership development, and financial guidelines, with subsidized production and shipping costs of such materials and trainings offered to smaller, newer, and under- resourced chapters; and be it Resolved, that this plan shall furthermore include the DSA national staff in cooperation with the Personnel Committee of the National Political Committee (NPC) to allocate dedicated staff support for the formation and development of new chapters and organizing committees especially in small cities, rural areas, and the Southern United States in coordination with Regional Organizations; and be it Resolved, that the NPC shall issue a report to the general membership by January 31, 2020, documenting their progress toward establishing the Growth and Development Committee.; and that a workable if not complete version of the plan to grow DSA to 100,000 members by January 1st, 2021 shall be presented to and ratified by the NPC by April 1st, 2020 and distributed to the membership; and be it Finally resolved, that the NPC through the Growth and Development Committee

[PDF page 11] Background Information shall distribute quarterly progress reports to the membership which shall include membership numbers, membership demographics, notice of newly formed chapters and organizing committees, and all other pertinent updates. Whereas, our main goal should be to develop DSA into a mass organization composed principally of workers, able to build power through labor, community, electoral, and movement organizing; and Whereas, to do so we must confront the organizational problems most pressing to us—the need to give all members a voice in our work; the need to grow beyond a predominantly male and white membership to a membership that better reflects the composition of the US working class; and the need to develop an organization able to build and effectively wield power; and Whereas, while our approach to anti-racist and socialist-feminist organizing should include commitments to creating welcoming spaces, we must also develop strategies to address the substantive and specific challenges black, brown, indigenous, and women workers face in the workplace and society at large; and Whereas, developing strategies to address these challenges must be informed by the knowledge and experience of those directly impacted, and shaped through their full participation and inclusion in decision making at every level of our organization;

[PDF page 12]

Resolution #17 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Record and Transcribe NPC Meetings” by Dag A. of Eugene DSA Staff Costs $789 Coordinating paid or volunteer sound engineer for each NPC meeting and transcriber for each NPC meeting and each steering committee meeting. 12% Overhead $95 Direct Costs $4,660 Sound equipment; paying sound engineer and transcriber if unable to arrange volunteers. Total Cost $5,544 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve • Equipment: ~$500 one-time expense • Recording engineer, if not volunteer: 16 hours per NPC meeting, $20/hr, 4 meetings annually: $960 annually • Transcriptions, if not volunteer: $50/hr, 12 hours per NPC meeting, 4 meetings annually, 2 hours per SC meeting, 26 meetings annually: $5000 annually The expense of this resolution would be very small compared to the DSA budget. Expenses can be drastically reduced by using volunteers for recording and transcription. Be it therefore resolved, the NPC and the NPC Steering Committee shall create transcriptions of all their meetings and other decisions, and additionally, audio or audio-visual recordings of in-person or teleconference meetings. Such recordings and transcriptions shall be made available to DSA membership within 7 days of the meeting, along with any documents used during the meeting. An online archive of these transcriptions and recordings shall be made available to members. Recordings and transcriptions of executive sessions and any documents exclusively used during executive session shall not be released to members, but shall be kept for archival purposes. Be it further resolved, best effort shall be made to ensure that every meeting participant can be fully understood in the recordings, including obtaining professional- quality recording equipment for in-person NPC meetings, and ensuring that someone trained in operating such equipment is present at each in-person NPC meeting. Staff are directed to consult with members for expertise and obtain equipment and services as needed to fulfill these requirements immediately. Be it further resolved, all national committees and subcommittees thereof shall release minutes of their meetings to members, and release records of any binding decision outside a meeting. Such minutes and records shall be released within 7 days of the meeting or decision.

[PDF page 36] Background Information Whereas, socialism requires a democratic process to bring power to the entire working class, and transparency and accountability are fundamental principles of democratic process; and Whereas, our members must understand how our leaders conduct themselves in order to hold them accountable; and Whereas, some members have disabilities preventing them from accessing the remote viewing of these meetings, or are otherwise unable to attend meetings in person or virtually, when their interests are being discussed;

[PDF page 37]

Resolution #19 Title & Author Budget Analysis “National Organizing Manual” by Dag A. of Eugene DSA. Staff Costs $1,435 The organizing staff work of updating our organizing manual is already planned with the hire of our new Organizing Director Glenn. However, should it be by committee there would be considerably more cost because of staff time to coordinate the committee. 12% Overhead $172 Direct Costs $250 Total Cost $1,857 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve • Website maintenance. ~ $250 annually for technical infrastructure. • Creating and migrating the content that Staff are responsible for may take several days of staff time depending on what is already available and how much volunteer labor can be used. Maintenance of content estimated at 1 hour monthly. Staff time used answering member questions may be reduced. Be it therefore resolved, the NPC shall, by January 1, 2020, establish a National Organizing Manual Editorial Committee. The National Organizing Manual Editorial Committee shall, by March 1, 2020, create an online National Organizing Manual, accessible to all members, to collect in one place the information that DSA organizers need to interface with DSA and to do their organizing work, and that provides a way for DSA members to share their work and document how others can replicate it. Members of DSA may contribute content to the National Organizing Manual that documents how to replicate campaigns, projects, and organizing tasks they have experience with. Members may also update content in the Manual written by other members, so as to improve it. Be it further resolved, the National Organizing Manual Editorial Committee may set policy regarding contributions to the Manual. All policies regarding Manual contributions will be made accessible within the Manual itself. At the discretion of the National Organizing Manual Editorial Committee, some Manual content regarding security concerns may be deemed sensitive and accessible only to elected leaders within DSA. Be it further resolved, by August 2, 2020, the following required content in the manual shall be completed: 1. National staff will be responsible for creating and maintaining manual content pertaining to communicating with National DSA, including, but not limited to: a. How to submit required updates to National DSA from DSA Locals regarding officer elections, bylaws changes, and finances,

[PDF page 39] Background Information b. How locals may obtain refreshed membership lists from National DSA, c. How locals may obtain dues sharing from National DSA, d. How to start a DSA local, e. How to start a DSA state or regional organization, f. How a member can update their membership information with National DSA, g. How to initiate a National Grievance process, h. How to make any other requests or updates to National DSA as mandated by the Convention or by the NPC. i. A list of National Staff and each of their roles and responsibilities j. An organizational chart of National Staff 2. National Working Groups will each be responsible for creating and maintaining manual content describing the scope of their work, projects the working group is working on, and how to communicate with the working group, including how to sign up for the working group and how to contact the working group. 3. National Committees will each be responsible for creating and maintaining manual content describing the scope of the committee’s work and how to contact the committee. 4. National Commissions will each be responsible for creating and maintaining manual content describing the scope of the Commission’s work, projects the Commission is working on, how to participate in the Commission, and how to contact the Commission. And be it finally resolved, the National Organizing Manual Editorial Committee shall liaise with staff, Working Groups, Committees and Commissions to ensure these requirements are met, and shall solicit contributions to the Manual from members. Whereas, collectively, our membership possesses enormous quantities of organizing knowledge, only some of which is written down, and which is not currently collected in one place, And whereas, new members and new chapters face a difficult task of learning how to do all the organizing tasks necessary for our work, And whereas, communication of processes is key to maintaining a cohesive and effective relationship between the National Organization and local leadership;

[PDF page 40]

Resolution #20 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Protection for Caucuses” by Ben C. of Portland DSA and Michael C. of NYC DSA. Staff Costs $8,035 Staff time if the 6 known ideological caucuses have same access to support as do national working groups (internal and external communications, website updates, Zoom calls, organizing coaching), plus for a national referendum (assuming the referendum resolution also passes: staff time setting up the vote, designing and ordering postcards for members who have no or non-working email address, cleaning and updating data, answering member inquiries. 12% Overhead $964 Direct Costs $7,885 Opavote fee + Mailed Ballots printing, handling and postage. Total Cost $16,884 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve As of this vote, voting for the entire membership should be implemented as part of the Membership Portal project, so cost should be minimal. Therefor be it resolved, all DSA members have the right to organize into caucuses based on shared identities, strategies, and ideological positions. A caucus is any such formalized free association of members at any level of the organization; and Be it further resolved, all caucuses should have the right to promote their events and initiatives in official DSA communications channels, like any other event organized by DSA members; and Be it further resolved, all caucuses should have the right to advocate openly for their views in the organization, no matter how small a minority they may comprise, unless their core positions or collective behavior can be shown to violate the core principles of the organization as articulated in the Constitution; and And be it finally resolved, a national Caucuses Working Group will be formed to explore the possibility of amending the DSA Constitution or Bylaws to solidify these protections. The working group will have a method by which members of DSA may join and will be democratically structured. It will work to one aim: presenting an amendment proposal to the national membership. Should the Caucuses Working Group finalize such a proposal by 11:59 pm Eastern Standard Time on December 31, 2020, ratification of the amendment will be decided by a referendum of the membership (with a quorum of four percent [4%] of the entire membership). Should this not be possible for any reason, the Convention Committee for the 2021 National Convention will place the question of the amendment on the order of the day for one of the dates of that convention.

[PDF page 41] Background Information WHEREAS, DSA is committed to multi-tendency organizing and embracing a spectrum of leftist thought and organizing spaces for people of every identity, but there are no guarantees that the National Office or chapter leaderships will allow individual caucuses to organize. To fully realize that commitment to ideological diversity on the left, caucuses’ right to organize must be protected;

[PDF page 42]

Resolution #23 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Updating DSA’s Harassment & Grievance Policy” by Abby A. & Laura C. of Chicago DSA; Ella M. of NYC DSA. Staff Costs $18,652 Some of this work is already in progress however we have inadequate staff time so additional staff time to coordinate - estimated 1/4 time staffer. 12% Overhead $2,238 Direct Costs $0 Total Cost $20,891 Resolve BE IT THEREFORE RESOLVED, that our Harassment Policy [Resolution 33 as adopted at the 2017 National Convention] be amended to include the following language, inserted in the indicated sections: [...] 2. Reporting Harassment [...] c. Reporting procedure and timeline [...] 4. Neutrality. If chapter HGOs and complainants agree that, depending on the circumstances of the grievance or parties involved, the local chapter steering committee cannot be neutral in administering the decision of the grievance, then the grievance decision process may be sent to a panel of 5-7 national HGOs randomly selected from a pool of HGOs. I. It is encouraged that members of local steering committees recuse themselves from final decision of grievances if they do not believe that can be neutral in the administration of the grievance decision. 5. Emergent circumstances. Grievances alleging rape, sexual assault, or physical assault, must be immediately notified to the National HGO Staff Member and can be elevated to the National HGO Committee and supervised by one trained staff member at any point if the chapter HGOs and complainants agree. [...] D. National HGO Committee a. The national HGO committee shall consist of all chapter HGOs, and any staff members charged with managing the National Grievance Procedure.

[PDF page 45] b. The committee shall undergo training required c. The committee is charged with creating all forms necessary for facilitating the grievance process and making them available to members. d. HGO’s shall recuse themselves from grievances that fall under the purview of Section C, 4 of this resolution if the grievance comes from their home chapter or they feel they are not in a position to neutrally review the case. [...] 3. Remedies and penalties [...] d. Appeals process. Either party may appeal the decision or form of relief determined by a local chapter Steering Committee to the National Grievance Officers by filling out an appellate form created by the National HGO Committee. Appeals must be filed within thirty days of receiving written notice of the Steering Committee’s decision. All appeals must be reviewed by the National HGO Committee as defined in Section D. The limited grounds for appeal are: i. Either party believes the behavior was not interpreted using the standards for harassment set out in Section 1a; ii. Procedural errors, misconduct, or conflicts of interest affected the fairness of the outcome; and iii. The remedy or penalty determined by a Steering Committee was grossly disproportionate to the violation committed. Background Information WHEREAS, in the two years since our last National Convention in 2017, experiences in local chapters have revealed serious holes in our National Harassment Policy, particularly regarding conflicts of interest and appeals. The final decision on most grievances is left up to local steering committees, members of which may have conflicts of interest that impact their decision-making; WHEREAS, there is no appeals process able to provide a check on potential conflicts of interest clearly stated in the current policy; and no assurance that appeals will receive adequate review from multiple neutral parties WHEREAS, ensuring safe spaces free of harassment and abuse, and protecting survivors, is essential to our organizing, and WHEREAS, DSA should commit the necessary resources to make our organization safe, and make sure that our procedures enshrine democratic processes and support our members;

[PDF page 46]

Resolution #24 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Adopting a Restorative Justice Model in DSA” by Melanie C. of Central NJ DSA. Staff Costs $18,652 Staff time to coordinate - estimated 1/4 time staffer to coordinate plus hiring second consultant for significant project.) 12% Overhead $2,238 Direct Costs $60,000 Professional training of NPC in restorative justice. Total Cost $80,891 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve Expand the role of National HGO; cost of professional training for NPC members; cost of sourcing and hosting materials on the DSA website. Background Information Be it therefore resolved, DSA shall publicly endorse restorative justice in our schools and communities; and Be it further resolved, at least 1/6th of members of the National Political Committee [“NPC”] shall receive formal restorative practices training by an certified instructor or institution with 6 months of the passing of this resolution; and Be it finally resolved, a team assembled by the RP-trained members of the NPC including the National Harassment and Grievance Officer, RP and RJ professionals, and member volunteers with specialization in this field, shall create a web-based Restorative Practices Curriculum and 5-year implementation plan that is accessible by membership within 1 year of the passing of this resolution, ensuring that resources for implementing RP are equally accessible by all local chapters and that the RP process is consistent and fair throughout DSA’s membership. Whereas our socialist vision for the future includes the abolition of police and prisons as well as the dismantling of the carceral state, as stated in Resolution 3 of the 2017 National Convention; and Whereas in place of incarceration, restorative justice [“RJ”] must be adopted to better address harm within a community. Schools and communities that adopt restorative justice models experience closer relationships, stronger feelings of fair treatment during harm reparation processes, greater feelings of safety, and a reduced likelihood to repeat harm; and Whereas it is imperative that socialists who advocate for a restorative society model positive restorative practices [“RP”] within their own organizing communities; and

[PDF page 47] Whereas a general lack of education surrounding restorative justice within DSA has led to a misuse of the concept in both good and bad faith by DSA chapters and perpetrators of harm, often at the expense of victims and relationships within the organization; and Whereas most efforts surrounding restorative justice are aimed at reacting to offense, we recognize that the only way to build a healthy environment is to proactively build a community where harm is less likely to occur; and Whereas RP provides a guide for improving connections among organizers, communicating with openness and clarity, reducing harm proactively, repairing harm post factum, healing damaged relationships, and developing meaningful bonds with new members;

[PDF page 48]

Resolution #26 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Creating the DSA Regional Coordinating Committee” by Suzanne-J. M., Trey D., & Joshua L. of New Orleans DSA: Tal L. of Los Angeles DSA. Staff Costs $37,305 Staff time to coordinate and support the RCC and regional bodies - at minimum 1/2 time staffer, though some of this work would also be done by the new Conventions and Conferences Coordinator Michael. 12% Overhead $4,477 Direct Costs $240,000 National Organizing Council meeting every other year, so annual cost would be 50% of the total estimated cost. 250 people, for purposes of averages say 125, covering all costs. Every year estimated 20 Regional bodies of 50 people (could be up to 100 people but use 50 as an average since some may be smaller) meeting once in person. Total Cost $281,781 Resolve THEREFOR BE IT RESOLVED, the National Political Committee shall establish a Regional Coordinating Committee. The NPC shall seek nominations for participation in the committee to ensure representation from small and rural chapters, and a range of geographic regions; and further Resolved, that the Regional Coordinating Committee shall be organized to: 1) Ensure that every Local is represented in a Regional Organization by August 2020. 2) Create a sample Regional Constitution and assist Locals, as necessary, in drafting Regional Constitutions and Regional Boundaries. 3) Assist in administering referenda to approve the new Organizations across regions and in mediating discussions regarding Regional Boundaries and Local assignment. 4) Determine the initial apportionment of Local delegates for each new regional organization, to be amended at the first Regional Meeting. Proportions for delegations shall be consistent within Regional Organizations but not necessarily between Regional Organizations; and be it Resolved, that the Regional Coordinating Committee shall be tasked to ensure that the Constitutions of Regional Organizations: 1) Include provisions to allow new and current Locals to leave and join adjacent Regional Organizations in order to facilitate more effective organizing. 2) Include provisions for the election of the Regional leadership body, to meet in-person or remotely, no fewer than four times annually. 3) Include provisions setting delegate apportionment for the Region annually, ensuring that all Locals are apportioned proportionally, that no Local shall receive less than than one delegate and one alternate, and keeping the total size of the Regional Delegation below 100 members. Delegates to the Regional Organizations shall be apportioned

[PDF page 51] Background Information based on the dues-paying membership of the Local (including all members up to one year arrears in dues); and be it Resolved, that the NPC shall ensure that once 50% plus one (1) of the total membership is represented on the National Organizing Council the powers and duties of the Regional Coordinating Committee shall pass to the NOC and the RCC shall be dissolved. Whereas, National bodies should be both accountable and effective, which at DSA’s current size requires creating intermediate democratic structures, and Whereas, National decisions should be based on concrete dialogue across local, regional, and national formations, and Whereas, coordination and cooperation between chapters within regions, and sensitivity to regional conditions more broadly enables our organization to respond to developments borne out of distinctive regional conditions;

[PDF page 52]

Resolution #28 Title & Author Budget Analysis “End Cash Bail” by Kansas City DSA Prison Abolition Working Group. Staff Costs $37,305 National priority - 1/2 time staffer. M4A did not have 1/2 of a staffer's time, each priority had less that that much staff time, but did have direct costs such as organizing materials, so to approximate those costs we allocate a 1/2 time staffer here. 12% Overhead $4,477 Direct Costs $0 Total Cost $41,781 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve Background Information This national campaign and committee should take up a similar amount of resources as the Medicare-For-All national campaign. Be it therefore resolved, the Democratic Socialists of America will begin a national campaign and form a national committee to promote the abolition of cash bail and pretrial incarceration via a variety of coordinated tactics, ranging from the promotion of community bail funds, to political education around transformative justice, and to advocacy for relevant proposals and legislation; and Be it further resolved, this project is not ancillary to other projects within the Democratic Socialists of America, but an integral part of the larger socialist vision for health justice and labor rights, which must be incorporated into existing political projects, not treated as niche concern separate from the core mission socialist politics. Whereas, the American political project is built on settler colonial genocide, chattel slavery, and fascist violence directed at people of oppressed nations; and Whereas, the Prison Industrial Complex (PIC) is one of the primary institutions by which the values of these systems (racism, cisheteropatriarchy, economic exploitation, etc.) are maintained; and Whereas, the movement to abolish prisons provides the only morally clear and workable framework to address not only the harm created by the PIC, but the harm at every level in our communities that takes the form of systemic and interpersonal violence; and Whereas, the abolitionist movement has identified abolishing cash bail and pretrial incarceration as a political priority in the near term capable of reducing incarceration rates and reigning in police power, instead of merely reforming the living conditions within prisons; and

[PDF page 55] Whereas, mainstream attempts to address “mass incarceration” risk increasing the powers of police and prisons, by focusing only on bail without addressing pretrial incarceration, inevitable allocating more money, increasing police presence, providing technology that can be co-opted by police and turned against the public, and rehabilitating the image of these institutions through promoting dialogues with individual “bad actors”;

[PDF page 56]

Resolution #29 Title & Author Budget Analysis Prioritizing Financial Solidarity” by Dag A. of Eugene DSA. Staff Costs $0 12% Overhead $0 Direct Costs $57,500 Costing out in resolution is incorrectly described. The regular registration rate for convention is highly subsidized (over 50%), not unsubsidized. There is no way to accurately estimate the impact on the budget with these conditions, so the estimate in the resolution is fine. Total Cost $57,500 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve For the 2019 Convention, the unsubsidized registration is $263, the cheapest housing is $120 unsubsidized, and a flight will cost around $300 on average. If half of delegates require a full subsidy and half can pay all costs on their own, financial assistance for the convention would cost approximately $341,500. If half of delegates received the maximum subsidy offered today, the cost would be $109,000. An additional fundraising effort is targeted at $175,000. The difference between these estimates is $57,500. Be it therefore resolved, When establishing the budget, the NPC shall prioritize ensuring that all members are able to participate in the National Convention and any other Constitutionally mandated events, regardless of the ability to pay, over program work, and shall include the cost of ensuring all members are able to participate, regardless of the ability to pay, in the budget for any program work. Be it further resolved, DSA shall establish a Financial Solidarity Program to enable all members to participate in DSA activities, including, but not limited to, the National Convention, regardless of ability to pay. Such program: • Shall minimize out-of-pocket costs to members requesting assistance; • Shall not require justification of need unless all participants in the event the assistance is for are asked to explain and reflect upon their class position. Be it further resolved, The NPC shall, by February 1 2020, establish a Committee to oversee and establish policy for the Financial Solidarity Program and a majority of such committee shall be DSA members that self-report that they are currently experiencing poverty.

[PDF page 57] Background Information Whereas, as socialists we stand in solidarity with people living in poverty; And whereas, we believe in democracy where decision-making power lies in the hands of ALL the people, not only those who can pay to exercise it; And whereas, DSA has many members experiencing poverty who should be just as able to participate in National DSA as those members who are financially secure; And whereas, means-testing assistance programs serves to humiliate those that benefit from them and deny them the help they need;

[PDF page 58]

Resolution #30 Title & Author Budget Analysis “National Grievance and Harassment Officer Expulsion List” by Forrest D. & Rachel R. of LA DSA Staff Costs 12% Overhead Direct Costs Total Cost The work of creating this list is already in progress with our national HGO Paula and Database Coordinator Eileen. Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve Background Information Staff time, particularly of the National Grievance and Harassment Officer, to respond to emails from Locals and Chapters and maintain a secure spreadsheet is estimated at no more than one hour per week or no more than 52 hours annually. Be it therefore resolved, the National Grievance and Harassment Officer be charged with maintaining a secure list of members of the national organization who have been expelled by Locals or Chapters. This list is to include the member’s name, the Local or Chapter from which this member was expelled, and the date upon which this expulsion was effective; and Be it further resolved that this list is to be populated by submissions from Locals and Chapters when judgements of expulsion have been made, rather than through periodic or proactive solicitation by the National Grievance and Harassment Officer; and Be it further resolved that this list is to be similarly updated by submissions from Locals and Chapters when judgements of expulsion have been overturned and are no longer applicable, resulting in the National Grievance and Harassment Officer removing the particular expulsion from the maintained list in its entirety; and Be it finally resolved that this list will be made available to the democratically elected and duly empowered leadership of any Local or Chapter that should formally submit a request to the National Grievance and Harassment Officer. Whereas individual Locals and Chapters of the national organization find themselves at the forefront of confronting issues of intra-member harassment and conflict; and Whereas these Locals and Chapters of the national organization are not in positions of authority to expel individual members from the entirety of the national organization; and Whereas the national organization has, as yet, not developed universal procedures for expulsion that can apply to all Locals and Chapters; and

[PDF page 59] Whereas this conflict could lead to unfortunate inaction;

[PDF page 60]

Resolution #35 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Establish a National BDS and Palestine Solidarity Working Group” by Olivia K. S., Jarib R., Valerie S. Staff Costs $861 Few hours per month, same as existing support to national working groups (internal and external communications, website updates, Zoom calls, organizing coaching.) 12% Overhead $103 Direct Costs Total Cost $964 Resolve Background Information Be it resolved that the Democratic Socialists of America establish a national working group dedicated to BDS and Palestine Solidarity; Be it resolved that the National will allow autonomous leadership and membership of said working group; Be it resolved that the National will reaffirm BDS and Palestine solidarity as a priority for the national organization and further commit itself to organizing in solidarity with the BDS movement, which urges nonviolent pressure on Israel until it meets three demands: ending its occupation and colonization of all Arab lands and dismantling the Wall; recognizing the fundamental rights of the Arab-Palestinian citizens of Israel to full equality; and respecting, protecting and promoting the rights of Palestinian refugees to return to their homes and properties as stipulated in UN Resolution 194. Whereas, the Democratic Socialists of America (DSA) endorsed the Boycott, Divestment, and Sanctions (BDS) movement at its 2017 national convention; Whereas, the DSA Internationalism Committee is a closed, application-based committee with a limited number of spots and thus cannot facilitate the kind of open collaboration among rank- and-file members that is necessary to advance BDS and Palestine solidarity work; Whereas, DSA working groups help link DSA to critical social movements and provide an important mechanism through which DSA members can mobilize around particular issues within the organization, and which anyone can become a member;

[PDF page 69] Whereas, a number of local DSA chapters are already involved in local and national level Palestine solidarity and BDS campaign organizing, in coordination with local and national Palestine solidarity organizations, and consolidation and coordination of these efforts would further develop and expand this work in DSA chapters;

[PDF page 70]

Resolution #36 Title & Author “Engaging with District Attorney Races to Fight Incarceration and Capitalist Power” by Emma C. of Brooklyn DSA, Alexandra H. S. of Queens DSA: David V. of Metro DC DSA; Dannelly R.; Ramsin C. of Chicago DSA Budget Analysis Staff Costs $1,220 Coordinating group to create materials, disseminating materials, providing organizing support a few hours a month. 12% Overhead $146 Direct Costs $0 Total Cost $1,366 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve $1,000 annually for staff time: sending materials to chapters that request it, processing endorsements BE IT THEREFORE RESOLVED that the National Political Committee (“NPC”), through whichever national body it deems fit, assists local chapters engaging in District Attorney (“DA”) and Attorney General (“AG”) elections, such as but not limited to providing messaging around decarceration, ending prosecutions of poverty, and prosecuting corporate wrongdoing. Be it therefore resolved that the National Office will designate a staffer who, upon requests from local chapters, will disseminate materials created by the NPC as outlined above, and that such designation can be incorporated, if deemed appropriate, into any broader responsibility of managing support of electoral efforts by chapters. Be it therefore resolved that through DA and AG elections, DSA will promote a socialist vision of prison abolition that protects people from corporate exploitation as well as dismantling racist incarceration and ending prosecutions of the working class. Be it therefore resolved that DSA, through DA and AG elections, promotes prosecuting corporations in all forms of illegal exploitation, including fraud, illegal menacing and surveillance of labor organizers, usury, violations of rent control, and wage theft. Be it therefore resolved that DSA, through DA and AG elections, advocates the end of prosecution of the working class in all forms, such as but not limited to welfare crimes, trespassing, and drug possession. Be it therefore resolved that DSA, through DA and AG elections, promotes public safety through means other than incarceration, policing, and surveillance of communities, such as but not limited to community mediation, harm reduction programs (such as needle

[PDF page 71] Background Information exchanges), and diversion programs that do not compel attendance by threat of incarceration. WHEREAS the United States has a two-tiered system of criminal law, where the wealthy and powerful avoid prosecution despite their exploitation and violence, and people of color and the working class are disproportionately prosecuted and incarcerated for crimes of poverty; Whereas elected District Attorneys (“DAs”) have unparalleled discretion to enforce or not enforce law, convene grand juries to conduct investigations, and prosecute corporations themselves as legal entities; Whereas the Black liberation movement in this country, from the #ByeAnita campaign of Assata’s Daughters to the Larry Krasner campaign led by Human Rights Coalition, has used engagement with DA races to fight for prison abolition and against racist prosecutions; Whereas the Democratic Socialists of America (DSA) has a valuable democratic socialist vision to bring to the prison abolition movement and needs concrete campaigns to lay out this vision; Whereas a politics of prison abolition rooted in forgiveness and humanity do not replace the need for accountability and systemic change, especially for the capitalists, landlords, and war criminals responsible for the majority of the harms in the world; Whereas the case of Maryland, where record decarceration has begun to be reversed by a violent new crime bill signed into law in 2018, shows that a window for fighting mass incarceration can quickly shut if not accompanied by a movement ready to build institutional power; Whereas it is important to call out opportunists, funded by corporate money, who will run for DA with promises of fighting mass incarceration but with no actual commitment to do so; Whereas coercive and punitive diversion programs like the Human Trafficking Intervention Courts in New York City which prosecutes sex workers for crimes rooted in class war, misogyny, and racism, show the risk of allowing the current political system to dictate decarceration;

[PDF page 72]

Resolution #37 Title & Author Budget Analysis “50/50 Dues Split” by Evan B., Kevin H., and Connor S. of Portland DSA. Staff Costs $8,609 50% loss of dues from annuals and 30% additional loss from monthly. 12% Overhead $1,033 Direct Costs $1,373,732* *50% loss of dues from annuals and 30% additional loss from monthly. Total Cost $1,383,374 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve Background Information Assuming all Locals contributing dues were incorporated and had bank accounts and that this resolution had been implemented in 2018, National would have received $841,338 in dues instead of $1,608,818 in 2019. National’s income after dues split (which includes $803,671 in contributions) would have been $1,819,852 instead of $2,623,523. Meanwhile, Locals would have received $841,338 instead of $73,858. In reality, not all Locals are incorporated with bank accounts so National’s post-dues split income would be greater than stated here. We anticipate that the NPC will have to rebudget for the upcoming year based on this resolution. All budget information in this Resolution is based on the DSA National Finance Call that took place on March 14th, 2019. Be it therefore resolved, that 50% of monthly dues contributions by members in the Local's jurisdiction shall be returned to that Local quarterly. Be it further resolved, that 50% of yearly dues contributions by members in the Local's jurisdiction shall be returned to that Local quarterly Be it finally resolved, that only Locals that are legally incorporated with a bank account will collect the dues split, as per current policy. Whereas National shared only 4.3% of total membership dues to the local chapters in 2018 (and the 2019 budget projected this percentage would go down) Whereas only monthly dues are subject to dues split, while yearly dues are kept entirely by National Whereas monthly dues accounted for only 21.9% of total dues collection in 2018 Whereas yearly dues accounted for 78.1% of total dues collection in 2018 and are entirely kept by National

[PDF page 73] Whereas many members do not know what is done with their money because they don’t see it benefitting them at the local level Whereas a grassroots organization will only be effective if it is equipping its Locals to organize with their own funds Whereas a 4.3% return of dues does not indicate a democratic grassroots organization but instead an issue-based advocacy organization;

[PDF page 74]

Resolution #38 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Fight for Abortion Access” Staff Costs $23,316 Coordinate campaign - 1/4 staffer. 12% Overhead $2,798 Direct Costs $0 Total Cost $26,113 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve Minimal materials costs; 600 hours of staff time to organize trainings and communications regarding abortion access as well as coordinate with electoral work and other groups, inside and outside DSA to advocate for abortion access. BE IT THEREFORE RESOLVED: 1. To have all federal, state, and local candidates endorsed by DSA vocally, enthusiastically, and unequivocally support abortion rights, access to abortion, the repeal of the Hyde amendment, and full federal funding of abortion. 2. To dedicate DSA resources to organizing the fight for abortion access, not only to oppose current efforts to criminalize abortion but also to eliminate all obstacles and expand access. These resources should include paid staff time, DSA communications, and development of training resources, which can include adoption and/or modification of existing training materials. These efforts should be undertaken in collaboration with DSA members who work in abortion care as well as with existing reproductive justice organizations such as the National Network of Abortion Funds and local abortion funds. 3. To declare that Medicare for All must include free reproductive healthcare for all people, including unrestricted access to abortion. 4. To frame our demand for free and unrestricted abortion within an overall vision grounded in reproductive justice. Consistent with the framework developed by Women of African Descent for Reproductive Justice and SisterSong, we envision reproductive justice to encompass our right to not have children, to have children, to be treated with dignity and respect in accessing all forms of reproductive healthcare freely and without restriction, and to be able to parent the children that we do have in safe and sustainable communities. We also advocate for centering and uplifting the marginalized voices of working class people of color that are most affected by abortion restrictions. 5. To challenge the dominant narrative that abortion should be “safe, legal, and rare” and replace it with the call for abortion to be fully funded and freely available without stigma.

[PDF page 75] Background Information WHEREAS: 1. Our socialism is feminist, anti-racist, anti-heterosexist, anti-cissexist, anti-ableist, pro-worker, and unabashedly supportive of everyone’s bodily autonomy without stigma or shame. 2. Abortion rights are under attack in the United States, both on the federal level and in the states. Within the first five months of 2019, draconian abortion bans have passed in Ohio, Kentucky, Mississippi, Missouri, Georgia, and Alabama. Once the newly passed laws are enacted, legal abortion will be dramatically limited or completely prohibited, either altogether (in AL), after six weeks from the first date of the last menstrual period (in OH, KY, MS, & GA), or after eight weeks (in MO). These attacks on reproductive freedom are increasing in frequency and intensity, and there is every indication that they will continue. 3. In many places where abortion is technically legal, financial and logistic barriers to accessing it creates an intolerable hardship for many members of the working class needing abortions. As long as abortion is treated as a separate category of healthcare, the difficulties inherent in navigating the legal restrictions, waiting periods, shortages of providers, and expense of abortions blatantly discriminates against people who are already oppressed by capitalism. This is felt even more keenly by incarcerated individuals who are or can become pregnant, who are routinely denied appropriate reproductive healthcare. 4. As abortion rights become further restricted and abortions become criminalized in several states, the impact of these injustices will be visited most severely on individuals who are already marginalized in our society. This means that our focus must address not only cis women who are centered within much advocacy for abortion rights, but also consider the additional risks of the carceral state for people of color, trans men and non-binary individuals, undocumented persons, sex workers, and disabled individuals, who may be at increased risk of incarceration and state violence where abortion is criminalized. 5. These attacks are grounded in misogyny, transphobia, and the desire to uphold a cis-heterosexual normative model of reproduction within “traditional marriage,” and we cannot rely on the courts to protect our reproductive freedom;

[PDF page 76]

Resolution #39 Title & Author “Petition to Bernie Sanders for a People’s Foreign Policy Platform” by Jared A. & David G. of Boston DSA: Carolyn D., Aram F., Roy Z., & Christian M. of Seattle DSA Budget Analysis Staff Costs $1,435 Social media work to make online petition successful to our internal and external audience. 12% Overhead $172 Direct Costs $1,000 Online ad buys for petition. Total Cost $2,607 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve Approximately 20 hours of staff time in 2019 to set up the petition, publicize to chapters and general membership, and redirect possible queries to the sponsors. Approximately 20-30 hours of staff time in 2020 for collecting the signatures, sending them to the Sanders campaign, and communicating the response back to the general membership. No expected cost other than staff time. Be it therefore resolved that the National Office will create an online petition urging Bernie Sanders to commit to the following actions immediately upon taking office: • Complete and immediate cutoff of all assistance to the Saudi military campaign in Yemen • Pledging to honor the letter and the spirit of the Iran nuclear deal while announcing a commitment to return the US as a signatory to the treaty • Suspension of all economic sanctions imposed by Donald Trump on Iran • Pledging to honor the letter and the spirit of the Paris climate accords • Reversal of the recognition of Jerusalem as the capital of Israel • Reversal of the recognition of the Golan Heights as Israeli territory • Restoration of funding to the United Nations Relief and Works Agency that was cut off by the Trump administration • Recognizing citizen economic boycotts as a form of free speech protected by the United States constitution, including the boycott, divestment, and sanctions movement for Palestinian rights • Dropping all charges against Edward Snowden, Julian Assange, and Chelsea Manning related to their whistleblowing activities • Restoring the diplomatic relationship with Cuba that was cut off by Donald Trump, and removing economic sanctions imposed on Cuba by the Trump administration • Removing all oil and banking sanctions imposed on Venezuela and supporting a negotiated solution to Venezuela’s political crisis • Introducing a Senate resolution to recognize the jurisdiction of the International Criminal Court (ICC)

[PDF page 77] Background Information • Removing all sanctions against ICC and its staff • Immediate cutoff of all military and security cooperation with Brazil and the Philippines until their governments demonstrate their respect for rule of law and human rights • Immediate repeal of the so-called Muslim ban Be it further resolved that the National Office will publicize the above online petition via printed materials, email, its web site and social media accounts, and will also encourage local chapters to do the same. Be it further resolved that at a future date determined at its discretion, the National Office will deliver the collected signatures to the Sanders 2020 campaign and request an official response, which the National Office will then communicate back to the DSA membership. Whereas large numbers of DSA members are actively supporting the Sanders 2020 campaign because of its progressive domestic policy agenda; Whereas socialism being inherently an international movement for workers' rights, the Democratic Socialists of America supports a just and anti-imperialist foreign policy; Whereas broad economic sanctions imposed by the United States on Iraq in the 1990’s and on Iran, Cuba, and Venezuela by the Trump Administration primarily hurt average citizens; Whereas a progressive foreign policy requires opposition to illegal wars and occupations, and other unjust power relationships;

[PDF page 78]

Resolution #40 Title & Author “Housing as a Human Right” by Donald J. and Stephanie T. of Austin DSA. Staff Costs $37,305 Appears to be priority campaign - 1/2 staffer. 12% Overhead $4,477 Direct Costs $10,000 Campaign materials. Total Cost $51,781 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve • Resolution would require staff time for supporting a national and local Socialist Housing Working Groups and Committees, including education, training, and campaign development; estimated 40-80 hours per month • Resolution would require expenditure of funds on educational and campaign materials; estimated $10,000 annually BE IT THEREFORE RESOLVED: 5. DSA declares that fair, safe, stable, accessible, and affordable housing is a human right; 6. DSA shall dedicate resources to support tenant organizing and education campaigns in cities across the country committed to the following: f) Promoting the decommodification of housing through expanding publicly-owned housing, social housing funds, and social housing supply; g) Developing solidarity networks dedicated to defending and expanding renters’ rights and supporting grassroots campaigns for tenant rights and empowerment; h) Resisting tenant evictions, slum conditions, exploitation, discrimination, gentrification, and displacement through collaboration with democratic renters’ organizations and helping organize tenant unions where they are not already present; i) Abolishing statutes and practices that criminalize homelessness and create barriers to housing such as criminal and eviction background checks; and, j) Defending, establishing, and expanding rent control. 7. DSA shall establish a Socialist Housing Working Group to help coordinate national and inter-local housing justice campaigns, coordinate and promote education on housing policy and socialist housing strategies, and support the creation or expansion of socialist housing local working groups, campaigns, and projects; 8. DSA shall require that any candidate seeking DSA electoral support should agree to the following, as applicable: k) Declare and advance Housing as a Human Right; l) Fund and build more public housing, social housing, rent-controlled housing, income-restricted housing, cooperative housing, transit-accessible housing, sustainable housing, accessible housing, and integrated housing; m) Fight for the elimination of evictions, displacement, gentrification, homelessness, criminalization of homelessness, discrimination based on class, race, country of origin, gender, sex, familial status, and LGBTQ status, discrimination based on criminal and eviction background, discrimination based on source of income (e.g., Housing Choice Vouchers), and sprawl;

[PDF page 79] n) Fight for rent control, tenants rights and tenant organizing, funding for affordable housing, housing and services for those experiencing homelessness, sustainable development, and non-exclusionary zoning; and o) Oppose measures that reduce or are contrary to any of the above. Background Information WHEREAS: 9. Housing is a human right essential to health, economic stability, and basic existence. 10. The working class will always face a housing crisis under capitalism and a private property regime. 11. The commodification and financialization of housing has created a crisis of affordability, exploitation, gentrification, and displacement. Housing costs are among the greatest direct burdens on the working class in cities across the United States; 12. The privatized system of American housing has failed to provide for the essential need of stable shelter for millions, leaving them debt burdened, in unsafe conditions, and increasingly precariously housed or homeless; 13. American housing policy has been crafted to reinforce segregation by race and class and to disempower and displace working class communities, especially those of color; 14. Public housing is deeply underfunded, with an estimated $40 billion dollars in public housing capital repair backlog and additional operational funding needed; 15. The chronic and growing unaffordability of American housing is exacerbating homelessness that the private market will never remedy, especially hurting economically-burdened families, the physically and mentally differently abled, and people forced to remain in abusive accommodations, in particular non-men; 16. Social housing models, especially publicly-owned housing, rent-controlled housing, housing cooperatives, and land trusts have proven effective at serving the needs of people above profit; 17. The People’s Policy Project has proposed a strategy for scaling up social housing by building 10 million new public housing units, built carbon neutral according to ecological principles, across the country (Link Here: Social Housing in the United States); and, 18. Compact, ecologically built housing with transit access can be a core industry to employ hundreds of thousands of unionized workers through a Green New Deal and is an essential piece of any comprehensive strategy to avert climate catastrophe in the coming decade.

[PDF page 80]

Resolution #41 Title & Author “Take the Fossil Fuel Energy Companies into Democratic Public Ownership Resolution for the DSA National Convention” by Philip L., Harris L., Ramy K., and Stuart S. of Seattle DSA Budget Analysis Staff Costs 12% Overhead Direct Costs Total Cost No additional up front costs. Resolve Background Information Be it resolved, as part of supporting and campaigning for the Green New Deal, DSA will advocate taking the major fossil fuel energy companies into democratic public ownership and to reallocate their massive resources into clean, renewable energy such as solar, wind, and geothermal; Be it further resolved, DSA demands that before any compensation is paid in the process of taking those corporations into democratic public ownership, the fossil fuel companies must be put on trial for the untold damage they have caused the planet and humanity and their systematic suppression of evidence of climate change. They must be held accountable, criminally and financially. Compensation should go to those with proven need, such as working-class and middle-class small shareholders; Be it further resolved, that DSA urges all its members elected to federal, state, and local political office to promote these demands; and Be it finally resolved, that DSA will promote these demands as part of its independent Democratic Socialists for Bernie campaign. WHEREAS Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez and others are proposing a Green New Deal that includes eliminating greenhouse gas emissions in the US by 2030, ensuring a “just transition” for the communities and workers currently employed by or dependent on the fossil fuel industry, and implementing major reforms like universal health care, affordable housing, and food security for all, especially communities of color; WHEREAS we recognize that the Green New Deal is incompatible with the power that fossil fuel capitalists have over our country and government, capitalists who are directly threatening the lives of billions of people and all life on earth; WHEREAS the major fossil fuel energy companies have amassed some of the greatest accumulation of power and capital in history which they will deploy to block the passage of a robust Green New Deal; WHEREAS “[J]ust 100 companies have been the source of more than 70% of the world’s greenhouse gas emissions since 1988,” as The Guardian reported (July 10, 2017);

[PDF page 81] WHEREAS Exxon’s own research in the 1970s revealed that fossil fuels contribute to climate change, and then oil companies intentionally suppressed this finding for decades; WHEREAS the Koch brothers are the owners of the second largest privately-owned corporation in the US, which made a large part of its profits through fossil fuels and they buy politicians like other people buy groceries; WHEREAS HuffPost reports: “The nine Democrats selected by House Speaker Nancy Pelosi (D-Calif.) to serve on the Select Committee on the Climate Crisis received a combined $198,000 from fossil fuel industry political action committees in the 2018 election cycle” (February 7, 2019); WHEREAS the most aggressive fossil fuel industry groups drove the US into two wars in Iraq, propping up the reactionary Saudi monarchy over decades, and attempting or carrying out coups in Venezuela, Iran, and countless other countries; WHEREAS we cannot control what we do not own;

[PDF page 82]

Resolution #46 Title & Author “National Amazon Worker Organizing” by Jamie P. of Portland OR DSA Budget Analysis Staff Costs $861 Few hours per month, same as existing support to national working groups (internal and external communications, website updates, Zoom calls, organizing coaching) OR incorporate into DSLC organizer if that resolution passes. 12% Overhead $103 Direct Costs $0 Total Cost $964 Resolve Background Information Be it therefore resolved, DSA, through the Democratic Socialist Labor Commission, will support the expansion and strengthening of the network of Amazon worker committees and will encourage salting and the formation of local solidarity committees through information sharing, training materials, publicity, research, and fundraising assistance. Whereas: “There is no strong socialist movement without a militant and powerful labor movement. DSA recognizes that if it is to become a socialist organization truly rooted in a multi-racial working class, it must expand its work both among unionized workers and among those currently without union representation”. – DSA Convention, 2017, and Whereas: 2018 saw the largest US strike wave in 30+ years. Democratic Socialists of America members were at the center of that organizing, are reinvigorating the labor movement, and many are looking for strategic opportunities to boost this upsurge, and Whereas: Logistics industry, like auto & steel in the 1930s, is today’s largely unorganized heartland. Logistics workers have their hands on the chokepoints in the global assembly line, and, especially in warehouses, concentrations of thousands of highly exploited workers are itching to resist, and Whereas: Amazon, at top of the logistics industry and rapidly expanding across the globe, plays a strategic role in the economy, and has been shown to be vulnerable to organized pressure. Amazon is the second largest US employer (after Walmart) with 300,000+ workers in over 300 warehouses, and Whereas: Amazon warehouses are most often located in inner-ring suburbs where Black and Brown workers (who have frequently been displaced from the inner city) reside and make up the majority of the workforce, and Whereas: Organizing in Amazon warehouses not only builds worker capacity to throw a wrench in the worldwide supply chain but uplifts other class struggles (eg. for housing,

[PDF page 89] schools, services) within racially and nationally oppressed communities surrounding these worksites, and Whereas: DSA chapters in Seattle and Portland have already launched Amazon workers organizing campaigns with members “salting” warehouses, initiating organizing committees, and engaging in collective action, with support from solidarity committees, and Whereas: DSA members in multiple cities, in concert with other left formations across the globe, are already developing a network of worker committees throughout the company;

[PDF page 90]

Resolution #47 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Regional Socialist Convergences” by Wayne R. of South Jersey DSA. Staff Costs $35,870 Resolution underestimates staff time needed for a conference even if staff role is merely coordinating and logistics. Also staff time processing applications and distributing checks from fund. 12% Overhead $4,304 Direct Costs $10,000 Cost of fund. Total Cost $50,174 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve • Funding: At least $500 for each event to be held • Organizer Time: at least 16 hours for each event to be held Be it therefore resolved, the NPC shall, by the end of 2019, establish a fund to encourage the growth of regional Socialist Convergences for educational and training purposes, held on either a subject of particular interest or for socialist education in general, of no less than $10,000. Be it further resolved, any local, local committee, or local working group, or any regional or state organization of DSA, may host a Socialist Convergence. The host group shall provide the National Office with the following details no less than 2 months before the Convergence: • The purpose and program of the Convergence, including potential panels and speakers; • A timeline and fundraising plan; • An estimated budget including the tentative location; • A code of conduct and set of community agreements; • A list of co-sponsors, including at least two of any of the following bodies of DSA members besides the host group: DSA locals, local committees or working groups, state organizations, or regional organizations. If the host is a local chapter, its committees and working groups do not count toward this minimum, and vice versa Be it further resolved, upon receipt of a plan for a Socialist Convergence which meets the above criteria, the National Office shall provide material support for the Convergence to include the following: • Financial support of not less than $500, with the option of further support. Such funding shall not be contingent on inclusion of particular programming or other conditions; • Publicity for the Convergence, including listing this on regular email communication and on a national calendar;

[PDF page 91] Background Information • Support from national or regional staff, before and during the Convergence, including (if possible) staff members attending and assisting with the Convergence if this is requested by the host group. Be it further resolved, National DSA as represented by its staff, officers, and at-large members of the National Political Committee shall have no formal role or decision- making authority in relation to the Convergence except as mandated by this resolution. Whereas, DSA has a need to improve its programs for internal education and regional cohesion, And Whereas, effort and funding put into educational events by local and regional organizations should be matched with assistance from the national organization, And Whereas, grassroots organizing of socialist gatherings for building skills and solidarity should be encouraged and fostered without needing to force these gatherings to adhere to any purpose outside that designated by the organizers, And Whereas, the Socialist Feminist Convergence held in Philadelphia in 2019 was an extremely inspirational event and should be held up as a model for future political education in DSA, And Whereas, the Afrosocialists and Socialists of Color Caucus is convening an excellent and inspirational convergence in New Orleans in June 2019;

[PDF page 92]

Resolution #49 Title & Author Budget Analysis “PAC Spending for Nationally Endorsed DSA Candidates” by Alex N. of Philly DSA. Staff Costs $74,610 Resolution misunderstands campaign finance law, but if amended and passed the work of administering the type of work in this resolution could be undertaken by the future Compliance Manager and Compliance Associate. Resolution still underestimates staff time needed - this would require hiring 1 full time staffer to assist the Development Director to fundraise. 12% Overhead $8,953 Direct Costs 0 Total Cost $83,563 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve 80 hours of Development Director time over 1 year, $500,000 in DSA’s national PAC NOW, THEREFORE, BE IT RESOLVED, that DSA commits to ensuring that nationally endorsed campaigns be conducted to the greatest extent possible in coordination with candidates through DSA’s PAC; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that DSA’s Development Director lead an organizing effort to raise at least $500,000 for DSA’s PAC for the 2020 electoral cycle by soliciting PAC contributions via phone and email from all of DSA’s members; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that nationally endorsed candidates be asked/encouraged to share all field, phone, fundraising, and text bank data with national DSA and all DSA chapters whose jurisdictions overlap the candidate’s district as a condition of receiving a national endorsement and concomitant PAC contributions; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that all federal candidates DSA endorses be given at least $2,000 in PAC funds except in cases where the NPC votes to support a candidate via an independent expenditure campaign instead of through coordinated spending; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that all state-level candidates DSA endorses be given either the maximum PAC contribution allowed by state law or $2,000, whichever is lower, except for in cases where the NPC votes to support a candidate via an independent expenditure campaign instead of through coordinated spending;

[PDF page 96] Background Information BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that the NPC be empowered to authorize PAC spending in excess of $2,000 and up to the maximum legal limit for any nationally endorsed candidate; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that the NPC be authorized to use DSA’s national treasury to hire staffers to administer DSA’s PAC spending; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that national endorsements only be given to candidates who advance DSA’s national campaigns, including Bernie Sanders’s 2020 presidential campaign; BE IT FINALLY RESOLVED, that DSA PAC funds shall only be spent on nationally endorsed candidates. WHEREAS, the electoral victories of DSA members like Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, Rashida Tlaib, Julia Salazar, Elizabeth Fiedler, Summer Lee, and Sara Innamorato raise the profile of democratic socialism; WHEREAS, electoral campaigns offer approachable organizing and skills-building opportunities for DSA members; WHEREAS, national endorsements for DSA candidates are a means of vetting the viability of local candidates and providing them with further exposure; WHEREAS, national endorsements ought to benefit both DSA and nationally endorsed candidates more than they do; WHEREAS, the relative insignificance of national endorsements is an effect of DSA’s underfunded and underutilized electoral infrastructure; WHEREAS, a viable long-term strategy for democratic socialist candidates will require a durable fundraising and coordination mechanism to ensure that Locals receive the necessary support for their nationally endorsed candidates; WHEREAS, DSA could leverage its 65,000 strong membership to improve this infrastructure to lend weight to our national endorsements, to provide genuine assistance to our nationally endorsed candidates, and thus to develop an apparatus to hold nationally endorsed candidates accountable to the organization; WHEREAS, DSA’s separate segregated fund (PAC) may be used to spend money in federal and state-level races; WHEREAS, in 2018 DSA PAC’s only disbursements were spent on bank fees;

[PDF page 97] WHEREAS, a well-funded and coordinated strategy for utilizing the DSA PAC would ease the burden on Local chapters which could rely on the national organization for funds without the need to independently organize ad hoc fundraising;

[PDF page 98]

Resolution #50 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Decolonization, Self-Determination, Anti-Imperialism” by Thomas G. and Austin G. of Richmond DSA: Michael I. and Jonathan F. of Honolulu DSA. Staff Costs $861 Few hours per month, same as existing support to national working groups (internal and external communications, website updates, Zoom calls, organizing coaching.) 12% Overhead $103 Direct Costs 0 Total Cost $964 Resolve Background Information THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED, DSA will commit to the full decolonization of all the occupied lands of the so-called USA: self determination and full sovereignty for Hawaiʻi; Puerto Rico; Amerika Sāmoa; Guåhån; Northern Mariana Islands; Virgin Islands; and to all Indigenous nations whose ancestral lands are within the USA’s current borders; and independence to all other overseas territories and dependencies controlled, occupied, or otherwise exploited by the USA. This commitment would include the following actions: • building a movement in solidarity with and alongside those oppressed peoples and their own political formations, conscious and respectful of their own leadership and organization, against all forms of colonialism and imperialism; • creating a Decolonization Working Group to facilitate anti-imperialist and decolonization politics within the organization; • advocating for necessary exemptions to the Jones Act, the overturning of the Insular Cases, and all other laws and legal rulings that maintain the colonial status quo; the release of all political prisoners of the State, incarcerated for their resistance to Empire; • forgiveness of all debts and reparations to the survivors of this exploitation, as well as their descendants, so as to allow the free political expression of the oppressed of these lands; and the total dissolution of the United States Empire. WHEREAS, the Democratic Socialists of America (DSA) is dedicated to a world free of exploitation and oppression leading to the liberation of all people from capitalism and imperialism. WHEREAS, the USA is a prison house of nations founded on genocide, built by slavery, maintained by exploitation, and expanded through conquest.

[PDF page 99] WHEREAS FURTHERMORE, the USA comprises many nations, recognized and unrecognized, including but not limited to: Indigenous nations within the continental US&#39;s current borders, Hawaiʻi, Puerto Rico, Amerika Sāmoa, Guåhån, the Northern Mariana Islands, the Virgin Islands, lands occupied by military bases and installations, and other so called “overseas territories” of the USA. WHEREAS, a full commitment to decolonization, deoccupation, demilitarization, and full repatriation of Indigenous lands is essential to ensuring a socially just and environmentally sustainable future;

[PDF page 100]

Resolution #51 Title & Author Budget Analysis “National DSA Code of Conduct” by Ella M. of NYC DSA Staff Costs $5,022 Publicize and project manage appeals to national using Resolution 33 or the non- resolution 33 Appeals process now adopted by the NPC. 12% Overhead $603 Direct Costs 0 Total Cost $5,624 Resolve Background Information BE IT RESOLVED that the Democratic Socialists of America will adopt and widely publish a National Code of Conduct that prohibits the following behaviors: • Physical or verbal intimidation such as yelling; insults; threats or blackmail of any kind and behaviors that occasion a reasonable fear of physical violence such as moving into others’ personal space, grabbing their arm, pointing at their face, physically blocking them from moving, etc.; • Stalking or unwanted contact outside of the normal course of DSA spaces, events, and organizing work; • Verbal or emotional abuse including name-calling, denigrating jokes or innuendo, and the display or circulation of written or graphic material of a harassing, degrading, or discriminatory nature; • Non-consensual sexual/romantic attention and touching; • Discriminatory harassment on the basis of sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, physical appearance, disability, race, color, religion, national origin, class, age, or profession. Discriminatory harassment and its appropriate reporting process is outlined in Resolution 33. • Disrespect for meeting facilitators or use of progressive stack; • Pressuring anyone to take mind-altering substances; • Lying about other members or spreading defamatory material about members; and • Sharing private communications without permission. BE IT FINALLY RESOLVED that redress for violations of this Code of Conduct may be sought under the process outlined in Sections (2.) and (3.) of the National Harassment Policy/Resolution 33 adopted at the 2017 National Convention, as well as any further grievance procedures adopted by a National Convention or the National Political Committee. WHEREAS, DSA relies on each one of its members in order to uphold an environment of mutual respect and solidarity;

[PDF page 101] WHEREAS, a set of commonly held commitments, ratified democratically, may facilitate a sense of shared responsibility for creating such an environment, while reducing individualized and biased interpretations of what constitutes unacceptable behavior; WHEREAS, the scope of prohibited behavior outlined in the Harassment Policy, or Resolution 33, passed at the 2017 National Convention is limited to “harassment on the basis of sex, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, physical appearance, disability, race, color, religion, national origin, class, age, or profession” and “other protected classes”; And WHEREAS in an organization of the size and complexity of DSA, behavior which goes beyond the scope of prohibited behavior in Resolution 33, but that nevertheless corrodes a healthy organizing environment, is likely to occur;

[PDF page 102]

Resolution #55 Title & Author “Resolution on Grassroots Fundraising and Small Chapter Growth” by Nate K. of Metro DC DSA; Travis D. of Knoxville DSA; Sam Lewis of NYC DSA. Budget Analysis Staff Costs $37,305 Some of this work is already planned with the hire of our new Tech and Data Manager Rebecca, Membership Services Associate Elizabeth, and Organizing Director Glenn, in addition to Development Director Alison. But significant staff time would be required to develop guide, administer chapter mentor program, and coordinate the whole project - estimated 1/2 time staffer more. 12% Overhead $4,477 Direct Costs $0 Printing is already in national budget. Dues sharing costs not listed because resolution says they should be increased but not by how much. Total Cost $41,781 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve Staff would work with the National Tech Committee to develop a monthly dues drive webpage, as well as set up payment processor support for ACH Direct Debit, potentially involving a redesign of the main dues page as well. Staff would create training materials and graphics for locals to use in local dues drives. Staff would spend time talking to locals about the dues drive and encouraging them to participate. Staff would work with volunteers to create an organizing committee / small chapter organizing manual and training materials for regional conferences. Staff would work with volunteers to establish a national mentorship program. This resolution establishes that chapters shall receive double the dues-share on their first 50 members. However, it does not set a firm dues-share rate. The intent is that as a part of this plan, the NPC should work with staff in committee to create cost-estimates for various dues-share rates, and then make a final proposal for a dues share increase. BE IT THEREFORE RESOLVED, that the National Political Committee, in consultation with staff, chapter leaders, and members, shall devise and implement a plan by February 1st, 2020 to support a national dues drive; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that this plan should focus on working with locals to engage in local dues drives and grassroots fundraising by signing up local active membership for monthly dues; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that as a part of this plan DSA shall make public a web page(s) specifically for people to sign up for membership with monthly dues, in which a person may indicate their chapter affiliation, choose a monthly dues amount, and choose a payment method with electronic funds transfer provided as an option;

[PDF page 108] Background Information BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that as a part of this plan, any chapter within the first three years of recognition shall receive double the dues sharing rate on the first 50 monthly sustaining members in that chapter, and DSA may evaluate how best to determine which members should be considered the first 50; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that DSA shall publish an organizing committee/small chapter organizing manual that includes guidance for conducting dues drives no later than February 1st, 2020; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that special programming for organizing committees and small chapters generally and rural organizing committees and chapters specifically be provided at regional conferences; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that DSA shall revive the national mentorship program and create a large chapter-OC/small chapter buddy program whereby volunteer mentors work with national staff to provide regular advice and support to remote organizing committees and small chapters; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that the convention calls on each DSA local to endeavor to participate in the dues drive to build a strong, sustainable national organization with well resourced locals; BE IT FINALLY RESOLVED, that as a part of this plan, the NPC shall consider raising the dues-share rate to further encourage chapters to participate in the dues drive. WHEREAS, in order to fund the organization's staff and resources while still representing working class people, DSA engages in grassroots fundraising and solicits low-dollar donations; WHEREAS, DSA benefits greatly from being inclusive of low-income members by offering a wide range of dues options and by waiving dues requirements when a member is unable to pay; WHEREAS, the implementation of a monthly dues system, dues splitting, and electronic funds transfers were all authorized at the 2017 DSA Convention; WHEREAS, most of DSA's members make one-time or annual dues payments, but many locals have active membership who are willing to make larger contributions through monthly dues; WHEREAS, DSA’s ambitious organizing goals require financial and staff support for strong locals and a strong national organization; WHEREAS, DSA should prioritize continued growth and devote additional resources to supporting new chapters;

[PDF page 109]

Resolution #56 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Ecosocialism as a Guiding Principle” by Michelle B. of Orlando DSA. Staff Costs - No additional up front costs - everything in resolution is already done by national EcoSocialist Working Group and national Immigrant Rights Working Group. 12% Overhead - Direct Costs - Total Cost - Resolve Background Information Be it resolved, The Democratic Socialists of America will include ecosocialism as a guiding principle in recognition that ecology and climate change are intertwined with all of our organizing and effect the working class on many levels, and will not compartmentalize it or relegate all ecosocialist work to a single working group. This includes the platform, About Us section on the website, and all other descriptive documents for the organization. Democratic Socialists of America will also develop and conduct virtual trainings and reading materials to educate membership about ecosocialism and facilitate building ecosocialist organizing at the state and local level. Be it further resolved, DSA will prioritize and support campaigns: • To support opening U.S. borders to Central Americans and other climate change refugees • To collectivize and bring under community control the energy infrastructure • To address unsustainable, unjust, and inefficient food systems • To recognize and combat instances of environmental racism Which use realistic and science based models of climate change to inform discussion Whereas, climate change is impacting and will continue to impact the working class significantly more than the ruling class and will continue to apply greater stress to people of color, people in the Global South, and our poorest comrades. Whereas, capitalism is the cause of climate change and environmental destruction and the movement to protect our planet is essential to the struggle to end capitalism. Whereas, in our current environment, no leftist organizing is relevant without the consideration of climate change and environmental destruction. Immigration, labor

[PDF page 110] protections, houselessness, food insecurity, environmental racism, and feminism all intersect with and cannot be separated from climate change and environmental destruction caused by capitalism. Whereas, current climate change discourse often distorts science-based models either to imply that climate change is minor and distant, or to imply that it is too late. These sentiments make effective environmental organizing feel pointless or hopeless. Whereas, many modern environmental movements focus on blaming the working class, pushing for corporate responsibility and consumerist solutions to climate change; all of which limits both the effectiveness of their tactics and their inclusion of marginalized groups. Whereas, the United States has also been the leading country in exploiting the Global South, distorting their economies and social structures and depriving them of the resources they need to adapt to climate change. Whereas we can create more effective organizing tactics in multiple areas of struggle by establishing ecosocialism as not just a separate form of organizing but instead as a core value integrated into all areas of our work;

[PDF page 111]

Resolution #65 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Affirming and Centering Struggles of Oppressed, Exploited and Erased Comrades” by Andrew P. of LA DSA. Staff Costs $2,583 Difficult to estimate all instances when staff time would be required to coordinate input process. 12% Overhead $310 Direct Costs $0 Total Cost $2,893 Resolve BE IT THEREFORE RESOLVED, That the DSA recognizes and acknowledges the vile nature of all forms of oppression and condemns all such behavior, especially with regards to the destructive institutions of patriarchy, slavery, and settler colonialism; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, That any member(s) who deals in harmful tropes during DSA activities should be confronted with the inappropriate nature of their behavior, as an act of reconciliation in line with the principles of transformative justice. After multiple such attempts, should the member(s) still fail to conduct themselves respectfully towards comrades, actions should be initiated in accordance with Article 33 for uncomradely and discriminatory behavior, with the possibility of excluding them from spaces if their rhetoric may cause harm to others; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, That when the National Political Committee (NPC) is seeking commentary upon proposals or discussing them itself, it shall use the principles of ‘progressive stack’1 or other appropriate method to give precedence to oppressed voices; furthermore, when discussing matters pertaining or of interest to oppressed groups, that representatives, caucuses, or NPC members of said group be given priority in the order of comment, and that the discussion be centered around their perspective and experience; furthermore; if it should put an item on its agenda relevant to an oppressed group, that it should proactively reach out to an extant caucus or the members of that group directly in a reasonably sufficient time frame to seek their comment upon the upcoming business, that it may be presented at the relevant meeting; BE IT FINALLY RESOLVED, that the NPC, DSA Staff, or other body should not interfere in the functioning of any caucus or group of members of oppressed classes, and that such groups be allowed to function according to their preferred internal structuring, insofar as they are not peddling any discriminatory policies that would undermine solidarity with other oppressed groups or belittling the struggles of others. Background Information WHEREAS Article 2 of the DSA Constitution affirms the rights of the oppressed of all stripes;

[PDF page 126] WHEREAS socialism can only truly stand with the abolition of all forms of bigotry and discrimination, and the leveling of privilege; WHEREAS these forms of bigotry and discrimination intersect and combine in our individual and collective lives in complex and surprising ways, such that no piecemeal approach can fully deconstruct the discriminatory lessons of our capitalist upbringing; WHEREAS it is our duty and responsibility to our oppressed comrades to create a space that is affirmative in their acceptance and inclusion within the DSA, and that this can only through a rigorous program of education, anti-oppressive facilitation, listening to lived experience and centering marginalized voices, can the DSA lay the groundwork of making its spaces accepting and inclusive for our less privileged comrades;

[PDF page 127]

Resolution #65b Title & Author Budget Analysis “Affirming and Accommodating Struggles of Oppressed, Exploited and Erased Comrades” by Andrew P. of LA DSA. Staff Costs 12% Overhead Direct Costs Total Cost Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve ~2 hours of staff time per month, to update names in the database. Background Information BE IT THEREFORE RESOLVED, that should a Caucus or group ask for specific accommodations for their identity, that the NPC and DSA Staff should give their proposal due consideration; BE IT FINALLY RESOLVED, that official DSA policy shall be to refrain from using a transgender (or pseudonymous) member’s “Dead Name” (current or former legal/birth name) in referring to them, instead favoring the person’s preferred name, in all communications, both electronic and in-person. WHEREAS Article 2 of the DSA Constitution affirms the rights of the oppressed of all stripes, including disabled and transgender comrades; WHEREAS socialism can only truly stand with the abolition of all forms of bigotry and discrimination, and the leveling of privilege, and ensuring no comrade is discriminated against for ability or preferred nomenclature; WHEREAS these forms of bigotry and discrimination intersect and combine in our individual and collective lives in complex and surprising ways, such that no piecemeal approach can fully deconstruct the discriminatory lessons of our capitalist upbringing; WHEREAS it is a basic principle of respect to refer to individuals in a manner in which they prefer, WHEREAS not accommodating comrades of varying ability levels induces a chilling effect on our democracy by marginalizing the voices of the disabled; WHEREAS it is our duty and responsibility to our oppressed comrades to create a space that is affirmative in their acceptance and inclusion within the DSA, and that this can only through a rigorous program of education, anti-oppressive facilitation, listening to

[PDF page 128] lived experience and centering marginalized voices, can the DSA lay the groundwork of making its spaces accepting and inclusive for our less privileged comrades;

[PDF page 129]

Resolution #66 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Prioritizing Labor” by Connor L. of Centre County DSA; Nicole E. of Silicon Valley DSA; Justin C. of NYC DSA; Mark F. of Central Connecticut DSA. Staff Costs $37,305 Appears to require priority campaign level support as exists now - 1/2 staffer. 12% Overhead $4,477 Direct Costs Total Cost $41,781 Resolve Background Information Therefore be it resolved, that the 2019 Convention directs the National Office, the National Political Committee (NPC), and the Democratic Socialist Labor Commission (DSLC) to continue prioritizing labor work, and therefore be it Resolved, that the labor priority will maintain our commitments to internal and external labor education, organizing the unorganized, supporting rank-and-file movements for union democracy, and building ties to existing unions and workers’ centers, and therefore be it Resolved, that our labor work continues to center demands for equality and nondiscrimination in all its forms and prioritizes the facilitation of a diverse but united workers’ movement, for a federal jobs guarantee, and a $15 minimum wage, and therefore be it Resolved, that the 2019 Convention directs that Locals should, where deemed feasible and desirable, create Labor Working Groups, and that full membership and voting rights in such groups shall be open to all DSA members in good standing, and therefore be it Resolved, that the 2019 Convention directs the DSLC to support, as requested and needed, the work of Local Chapter Labor Working Groups, and therefore be it Resolved, that the 2019 Convention directs the allocation of staff time and monetary resources, as determined by the National Office and National Political Committee, including the possibility of hiring a full time labor organizer to support our efforts to organize the unorganized, and to supporting a labor priority, as modified by the relevant provisions of any collective bargaining agreements. Whereas, labor was designated a priority of Democratic Socialists of America (DSA) at the 2017 Convention in Chicago, and Whereas, as socialists the organized working-class, often represented through labor unions, is at the core of our emancipatory mission, and

[PDF page 130] Whereas, a militant, rank-and-file labor movement is necessary to advance socialist politics in the United States;

[PDF page 131]

Resolution #68 Title & Author Budget Analysis “A New Operation Dixie” by Austin G. (Richmond), Connor L. (Centre County), Douglas W. (At-Large, Maryland), Tawny T. (New York City, North Brooklyn) Staff Costs $37,305 Appears to require priority campaign level support as exists now - 1/2 staffer. 12% Overhead $4,477 Direct Costs Total Cost $41,781 Resolve Background Information Therefore be it Resolved, that the 2019 Convention of Democratic Socialists of America (DSA) calls on labor unions to prioritize a new “Operation Dixie” to organize the South, defined as the states of the former Confederacy, and therefore be it Resolved, that the 2019 Convention directs the National Office of DSA to prioritize Southern chapters in providing organizational support for new labor organizing, and therefore be it Resolved, that the 2019 Convention directs the National Political Committee (NPC) to prioritize organizational support for labor organizing in the South, whether under the auspices of DSA or otherwise, with a particular emphasis on working with unions with records of proven organizing success, and therefore be it Resolved, that the 2019 Convention directs the National Office to ensure that all chapters in the South will be provided with ongoing staff support in any labor organizing endeavors, and that all Southern chapters will be provided with the opportunity for in- depth training on new organizing by August of 2020, and therefore be it Resolved, that the 2019 Convention directs the National Office to earmark staff time to support enacting this Resolution, as modified by the provisions of any relevant collective bargaining agreements. Whereas, the South has historically proven a bulwark of reaction built upon the rule of capital and structural racism, and Whereas, Southern reactionaries have proven among the most implacable enemies of the American working-class, and Whereas, low union density in the South is a threat to organized labor and the entire working-class, and a critical barrier to socialist power, and

[PDF page 133] Whereas, the cure for the system of racism and white supremacy stretching to all corners of the nation is still principally found in the South, and Whereas, the movements of teachers, autoworkers, fast food workers, university workers and other groups of workers in the South over the past five years have provided a model for multi-racial, and in some cases explicitly feminist and antiracist, labor organizing, and Whereas, class struggle and antiracist issues such as teacher-to-student ratios and the school-to-prison pipeline and feminist issues such as low pay for teachers are both broadly felt issues and demands around which workers in the South are already organizing;

[PDF page 134]

Resolution #72 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Mass Campaign for Voting Rights” by Ashley P. & Shane R. of East Bay DSA. Staff Costs $149,219 Resolution calls for 2 full time staff. 12% Overhead $17,906 Direct Costs $60,000 Difficult to estimate for 2 attorneys on retainer, though incoming Compliance Manager may be able to provide support around state election laws. Total Cost $227,126 Author’s Cost Estimate Resolve We recommend DSA hire at minimum two lawyers on retainer with expertise in these areas and two full-time DSA field staff to support chapters in carrying out this campaign. Therefore be it resolved as part of DSA’s anti-racist struggle we should embark on a mass campaign for voting rights, Therefore be it resolved that DSA should create a national campaign committee standardizing the criteria for voting rights and access across all 50 states including but not limited to: • Restoring and expanding Section 5 of the 1965 Civil Rights Act • Requiring International Observers in US Elections • Pushing for independent voting district commissions in each state • Ending Felon Disenfranchisement • Election Day National Holiday, Automatic voter registration, and Vote-by-Mail • Eliminating Voter ID Laws • Early Voting including the two weekend before the elections • Lowering Ballot Access Thresholds for 3rd Parties and encouraging Rank-Choice Voting Therefore be it resolved that DSA will provide support in each state to pursue a task force to identify where they fall short of the vision above and provide resources to its chapters to meet these criteria and create campaigns around them including video, social media, paper literature, political education events, and election day support, Therefore be it resolved DSA should tie this campaign into a process to naturalize undocumented immigrants immediately and to offer citizenship to all undocumented immigrants who seek it,

[PDF page 141] Background Information Therefore be it resolved, DSA chapters will provide material support to voters on election day where lengthy lines exist to ensure no one is deterred from exhaustion or disability. DSA chapters should serve as election observers, escort marginalized groups to the polls and report intimidation tactics such as racist vigilantes; Whereas the integrity of the vote is an essential aspect of mass political participation and a necessary precondition to form a real workers’ party in the US, Whereas a democratic state poses a major threat to the political domination of capital, Whereas democratic states have been a historic demand and hard-fought victory of working class movements across the globe, Whereas as the historic union between a Capitalist economies and liberal democracies has been challenged by the rise of right wing parties internationally, Whereas voter suppression tactics have a disproportionate impact on the most marginalized communities, especially the African-American community, Whereas the gains made by the Civil Rights Movement have been eroding for decades culminating in Shelby County v. Holder decision to strike down Section 5 of the Voting Rights Act;

[PDF page 142]

Resolution #73 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Support for Open Borders” by Elijah S., Marvin G., Madi M., & Eva L. S. of NYC DSA; Brandon R. R. of DSA Los Angeles. Staff Costs $1,076 Coordinating and compiling information from across the country. 12% Overhead $129 Direct Costs $0 Total Cost $1,205 Resolve Be it resolved that DSA supports the demand for open borders; Be it resolved that DSA supports the uninhibited transnational free movement of people, the demilitarization of the US-Mexico border, the abolition of ICE and CPB without replacement, decriminalization of immigration, full amnesty for all asylum seekers and a pathway to citizenship for all non-citizen residents; Be it resolved that DSA develops political education resources to be shared with chapters across the country to deepen and broaden the understanding of the demand for open borders and how to fight for it. These efforts shall be made in coordination between the national Immigrants’ Rights Working Group, the International Committee, and chapters and members across the country working on issues of immigration justice and internationalism; Be it resolved that DSA recognizes and reflects our support for open borders in our evaluations and endorsement of political campaigns. Background Information Whereas Democratic Socialists of America (DSA) members across the country organize on a daily basis for anti-imperialism, immigrant justice, labor rights, ecosocialism, and many other issues, with a core belief in solidarity between the workers of the world and an international socialist movement; Whereas DSA is an internationalist socialist organization, and as such it recognizes that socialists fight to build working class power for all people, regardless of immigration status or country of residence; Whereas borders serve to undermine the international working class, by pitting immigrant and citizen workers in America — and foreign and American workers — against each other in a race to the bottom; Whereas border and immigration enforcement are tools of white supremacy, capitalism and imperialism; Whereas borders, their creation, and their enforcement, erase the existence and sovereignty of indigenous peoples;

[PDF page 143] Whereas borders are, and will increasingly be, utilized to violently dominate and restrict people’s movement as climate change pushes people from their homes; Whereas border and interior immigration enforcement threaten immigrant workers’ ability to organize; Whereas Comprehensive Immigration Reform policies seek to control who “deserves” entrance to and/ or permanence in the US, and offer empty promises of protection for resident immigrants in exchange for increased border enforcement, and thus will continue to undermine solidarity and organizing efforts for immigrant justice; Whereas immigration policy, and its enforcement practices, are set on the federal level, without democratic input or accountability and thus demands a nationally coordinated response; Whereas the continual assault on immigrants’ rights from both the right and the center produces a reactionary, short-sighted view on immigration that reinforces border securitization, which pervades even leftist spaces and thus necessitates a positive, radical vision for immigration justice and internationalism;

[PDF page 144]

Resolution #75 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Organize Rallies and Strikes to Defend Womxns’ Rights” by Harris L. (Seattle), Evan S. (Boston), Bianca D. (Columbus). Staff Costs $1,722 Not listed in resolution, but estimate a few hours per month, somewhat more than existing support to national working groups (internal and external communications, website updates, Zoom calls, organizing coaching.) 12% Overhead $207 Direct Costs $0 Total Cost $1,928 Resolve BE IT RESOLVED, that when the Supreme Court agrees to review an abortion case that threatens the fundamental right to abortion, the national DSA leadership in collaboration with local DSA chapters and in alliance with other progressive organizations will contribute and initiate where needed to urgently organize national days of action, including the call for feminist strikes and mass protests, as the “Mass Strike for Reproductive Justice” resolution by Jen James, Tiffany Berruti, Natalie Adler, Ximena B. and Ena Lee outlines, to take place in as many cities possible; BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that DSA will work with the Womxn’s March organization and reach out to other progressive forces such as Planned Parenthood, NARAL, NOW, unions, and Our Revolution, to organize mass marches in January 2020, working to reverse the decline in participation over the last two years. If no marches are organized in any given city by larger groups, we encourage local DSA chapters to take the lead in organizing them; and BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, DSA seeks to help build a socialist feminist wing within the broader feminist movement. DSA encourages local chapters to organize public meetings shortly before or after the marches to popularize socialist feminism, as well as the following demands: • Medicare for All, including reproductive healthcare, birth control, abortion, and gender confirming medical care, free at the point of use for all residents in the US • Universal, high quality, public childcare and pre-K • 24 weeks paid parental leave • Mandatory scientific sex education in public schools, with a focus on consent and LGBTQ+ normalization • The establishment of a federal OSHA-style office that is fully empowered to investigate and take action against workplace sexual harassment. Any worker will be able to report workplace harassment to this office, which will be

[PDF page 145] Background Information completely independent of their employer. While federally funded, investigations will be carried out by unions and gender justice organizations. BE IT FURTHER RESOLVED, that DSA urges all its members elected to federal, state, and local political office to use their office and access to the media to build and promote these rallies, strikes, and demands; and BE IT FINALLY RESOLVED, that DSA will promote these rallies and demands as part of its independent Democratic Socialists for Bernie campaign, and will urge Bernie to promote these rallies, strikes, and demands as well. WHEREAS at least 13 states passed laws this year restricting abortion, including Alabama where almost all abortion is banned without exceptions for rape or incest, and several states have passed “heartbeat” laws, and it is extremely likely that the abortion bans will be taken to the Supreme Court to challenge Roe v Wade; WHEREAS Womxn, LGBTQ people, and feminists are fighting back through the online #MeToo movement, workplace walkouts to protest harassment, protests against abortion bans, and millions of people around the world participated in feminist strikes in the last three years; WHEREAS Roe v Wade was decided by a Supreme Court with a majority of judges appointed by Republican presidents because there was a mass feminist movement and other movements demanding much more;

[PDF page 146]

Resolution #77 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Supporting Workers Organizing in Electoral Campaigns” by Ronald J. of Philly DSA. Staff Costs - No additional up front costs. 12% Overhead - Direct Costs - Total Cost - Resolve Background Information Be it therefore resolved, that DSA will support the organizing efforts of electoral campaign workers and stand in solidarity with them; Be it further resolved that, DSA will only endorse and support electoral campaigns - candidate-centric and issue-based - whose management will stay neutral or be supportive if their workers attempt to organize; Be it further resolved that, DSA will revoke any endorsements made and support committed for an electoral campaign if evidence emerges that its management organized and acted to prevent their workers from organizing; and Be it finally resolved that, DSA will work to persuade allied political organizations to adopt for their own organizations the standards outlined in this resolution, which are: 1. supporting the organizing efforts of and standing in solidarity with electoral campaign workers; 2. demanding electoral campaigns seeking their endorsements and support to stay neutral or be supportive if their workers attempt to organize; and 3. revoking any endorsements made and support committed for an electoral campaign if evidence emerges that its management organized and acted to prevent their workers from organizing. Whereas, workers who work on electoral campaigns - candidate-centered and issue- based - are organizing for higher wages, good benefits, better harassment guidelines and enforcement, fair contracts, and other rights; Whereas, the vast majority of workers on electoral campaigns are still forced to endure exploitative work conditions characterized by long hours, low pay, lengthy periods of material insecurity, and lack of accountability for harassment and abuse; Whereas, the management of many electoral campaigns do not address the grueling work culture that often persists in electoral campaigns;

[PDF page 149] Whereas, the harmful effects - being overworked, harassed, blacklisted, and so on - of the work culture of many electoral campaigns disproportionately fall on workers who come from low-income and/or marginalized backgrounds, particularly women; Whereas, the work environments of most electoral campaigns lead to many workers in this sector burning out and exiting the field of political and electoral organizing, thus draining political campaigns and movements - including those that Democratic Socialists of America (DSA) is invested in - of their expertise and experience; and Whereas, electoral campaign workers constitute a strategic sector for DSA to support as workers in this sector bring with them extensive experience and expertise in electoral campaigning and political organizing in addition to connections with broader political forces in the Left and its progressive and labor allies;

[PDF page 150]

Resolution #84 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Socialist Organizer Trainings” by Jane S. (Detroit), Bianca C. (NYC), Kristian H. (Dallas), Dave P. (Austin), Hannah A. (Lawrence, KS), Beth H. (Boston) Staff Costs - No additional up front costs. 12% Overhead - Direct Costs - Total Cost - Resolve Background Information Therefore be it resolved that we should continue to have national staff widely and consistently implement these two sets of trainings: the basic socialist organizer and chapter development training and the intermediate socialist organizer training for chapter leaders. Therefore be it resolved that we should continue and expand national staff support for nationwide training projects like last year’s AfroSocialist and People of Color Caucus training, with the intention of supporting and strengthening AFROSOC and other identity based caucuses in DSA. Therefore be it resolved that national staff should continue developing these mass organizing trainings in consultation with the national political education committee, the DSLC, the national electoral committee and other national working groups as well as the outside institutions whose materials staff have already drawn from to develop the first two sets of training (Labor Notes, Jobs with Justice, Midwest Academy and others). Therefore be it resolved that national staff should continue the development of the companion handbook, with skills-based activities and supplementary readings, for the trainings. Whereas, as an organization we seek to build the capacity of working-class people to take collective action based upon our mutual self-interest and shared values. Whereas collective action, especially through escalating campaigns, is the foundation for solidaristic social movements. These movements break down the individualism created by neoliberalism and social isolation under capitalism, white supremacy, and patriarchy create. Whereas we win material change through “campaigns,” a series of focused attacks against specific capitalist class actors to win specific changes in material conditions. Campaigns can target a landlord, employer, corporation, industry, city agency, university administration, state legislature, or national politician, and tactics could

[PDF page 160] include labor and tenant organizing, protest mobilization, electoral challenges, media work, and direct action. We distinguish the campaign from other forms of political activity through its discipline and specificity. A campaign demands a particular concession from a target and is grounded in a realistic power analysis that offers a path to winning. Whereas we have seen over the last two years that organizing skills training, grounded in this materialist analysis, has helped socialist organizers in DSA develop and share the skills necessary to wage strategic campaigns and build our political organization. Whereas, we test the strength of our organization when we set concrete goals (e.g. turnout to events, number of doors knocked, new members recruited) and engage working class people one-on-one to reach that goal. With this organizing model, everything we do becomes an opportunity to build and test our strength — it also fosters deeper connections between members through face-to-face conversation. Every single thing we do is an opportunity to train our members, recruit new people, test our structure and hold each other accountable. Whereas our staff have already developed trainings in the mass organizing model to help chapters build their organizing skills and infrastructure as well as their political capacities (e.g., how to structure debate, formally elect leadership, and make decisions democratically) and chapter leaders have found these trainings to be effective in growing and developing their local organizations;

[PDF page 161]

Resolution #85 Title & Author Budget Analysis “Standardize Accountability” by Elisabeth L. and Johannes M. (Silicone Valley); Sauce (North Bay); Aaron M. (Metro DC). Staff Costs $7,461 Some of this work is in progress with the expansion of time for chapter pipeline work by our Office Coordinator Lisa, but resolution underestimates staff time to maintain updated information from 175 chapters so a more realistic estimate is here. 12% Overhead $895 Direct Costs - Election software is not yet available so cannot estimate cost. Total Cost $8,356 Author’s Cost Estimate Adopting the Standard New Local Constitution should not incur additional costs, as the Chapter Pipeline Committee is already responsible for guiding Organizing Committees through the initial bylaws process. The National Organization should already be keeping an up-to-date reference of local Constitutions; to the extent this is not already being done, we estimate an initial 4 hours of staff or volunteer time needed to notify chapters to submit their current local Constitutions, we further estimate 8 hours to compile a list of local Constitutions and make it available to members via a digital medium such as the National forums, and finally 2 hours monthly on an ongoing basis to update that information when notified of any changes from Locals. For election software we expect a substantial amount of volunteer tech labor needed but minimal costs for hosting, on the order of $5 to $25 monthly. Resolve Therefore be it resolved that the text provided as an Appendix to this resolution is adopted as the Standard New Local Constitution referred to in Article 3, Section 2 of the DSA Bylaws; and Be it further resolved that certain parts of the Standard New Local Constitution (text within square brackets, Articles or Sections marked [OPTIONAL], and sections where footnotes indicate the possible need for alterations) shall be treated as recommended language that Locals may opt to leave out or modify; and Be it further resolved that Locals may include additional language as needed; and Be it further resolved that the NPC shall not alter the Standard New Local Constitution except to comply with decisions of the National Convention, or if legally necessary; and that DSA shall make available to all members a specific, detailed written rationale if alterations are made; and

[PDF page 162] Background Information Be it further resolved that DSA cannot and shall not impose requirements on any subsequent Local Constitutions ratified by the Local’s members, except any provisions that may be required by the DSA Constitution and Bylaws or applicable law; and Be it further resolved that in the case DSA chooses to make recommendations on language in Local Constitutions beyond the Standard (for example, to provide guidance on group tax exemptions), DSA shall notify Locals that such recommendations are strictly optional, and make available to all members a specific, detailed written rationale for each recommendation; and Be it further resolved that the text of all current Local Constitutions shall be made available to all members; and Be it further resolved that the National Organization shall, at no cost to Locals, provide election software that implements the recommended method(s) in the Standard New Local Constitution for use in Local elections. Whereas the DSA Constitution mandates that “The NPC shall produce a Standard New Local Constitution by which all newly chartered Locals shall operate;” and Whereas in late 2017, the NPC announced a draft Standard New Local Constitution that was irreconcilable with the values and existing practices of DSA; and Whereas the NPC is subordinate to the National Convention, the highest governing body of DSA; and Whereas the DSA Accountability Task Force, an informal grouping that was active in 2017 and open to all DSA members, on short notice organized a collaborative, bottom- up process to write a new set of sample bylaws, drawing on best practices from chapters across the country; and Whereas the Accountability Sample Bylaws are an expression of the democratic values and practices of DSA and are well-suited as the basis for the Standard New Local Constitution;

[PDF page 163]
